WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:21.900
THE FALL:
Not too long ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:28.740
Sam Bankman Fried, the founder of FTX, was the&nbsp;
richest man under age of 30. He had a net worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:35.340
as high as $27 billion, and he was even making&nbsp;
moves to potentially acquire Robinhood. Likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.720
the most impressive part about his success though&nbsp;
was that he was one of the few billionaires that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.600
was able to maintain a relatively positive public&nbsp;
image. Popular finance YouTubers like Meet Kevin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.720
Andrei Jikh, Financial Education Jeremy, and&nbsp;
even Graham Stephan constantly pitched FTX as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:57.180
the go to crypto exchange. They described Sam&nbsp;
as a philanthropic hippie who just wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.400
help people, and I fed into this perception&nbsp;
as well. While I’ve never done a sponsorship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.520
with FTX or anyone else for that matter, I did&nbsp;
post a rather positive video about Sam Bankman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.640
exactly a year ago. And let’s just say Sam was&nbsp;
great at playing this philanthropic monk role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.500
He didn’t live in some sort of California&nbsp;
estate with his fellow tech billionaires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.740
Instead, he lived in the Bahamas with 10 roommates&nbsp;
and drove a Toyota Corolla. But, likely the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.840
appealing feature of Sam for many was what he was&nbsp;
planning to do with all his money. From the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.720
beginning, Sam made it clear that his primary goal&nbsp;
with making money was to give it away. He lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:43.200
by this ideology of earning to give which was no&nbsp;
doubt extremely popular with the masses. But, at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.440
this point, Sam doesn’t exactly have much to give&nbsp;
because the vast majority of his fortune literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.780
got Thanos snapped. In fact, Forbes estimates&nbsp;
that Sam is no longer even a billionaire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.420
and the majority of his net worth today likely&nbsp;
actually comes from his Robinhood stake. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.540
how did things U turn so quickly? Well, let’s&nbsp;
just say that Sam went from promising to bailout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:13.020
everyone with his philanthropy to needing an $8&nbsp;
billion bailout himself for FTX. Now, to be clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:19.200
FTX says that only the international branch of FTX&nbsp;
is under stress and that the US branch is fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:26.520
but take that for what you will. According to&nbsp;
Forbes, FTX is now worth 0, Sam’s trading firm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:32.040
Alameda research is worth 0, and FTX.US,&nbsp;
well at the time of making this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.480
they say its valuation is unclear.&nbsp;
But, by the time you’re watching this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.420
this could very well be 0 as well. So, here’s&nbsp;
how Sam Bankman went from being the crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:48.660
superstar who made $27 billion in 4 years to&nbsp;
losing 94% of his net worth in a single day.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.300
NONBELIEVER:
If you’ve only been following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:01.080
media headlines, FTX’s liquidity crisis likely&nbsp;
came as a surprise. One day, FTX was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.480
most trusted crypto exchanges in the world, and&nbsp;
the next day, they suddenly needed a bailout. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.300
in reality, the FTX situation has been developing&nbsp;
for years and can be traced back to the origins of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:18.060
the company. If you take a look at why Sam founded&nbsp;
FTX in the first place, you’ll see that it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:24.660
due to a strong belief in crypto or bitcoin. In&nbsp;
fact, Sam is pretty neutral on crypto as a whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:31.020
The reason he started FTX was because he saw&nbsp;
opportunity. You see, before starting FTX, Sam was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.460
a traditional wall street professional working at&nbsp;
a trading firm called Jane Street Capital. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:41.640
he specialized in trading international ETFs,&nbsp;
but in 2017, he spotted an opportunity to trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:47.220
something much more profitable: Bitcoin. At the&nbsp;
time, there weren’t dozens of crypto exchanges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.260
that were all super well funded and had millions&nbsp;
of users each. Most people who were into crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:57.480
preferred to keep the majority of their holdings&nbsp;
in cold storage wallets and only used exchanges to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.520
buy and sell crypto. This led to relatively&nbsp;
low volume across many exchanges leading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:09.060
discrepancies in price. On one exchange Bitcoin&nbsp;
might cost $10,000 while on another it might cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.920
$10,100. If you were willing to buy it on the&nbsp;
first exchange, transfer it over to the second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:20.100
and then sell it, you’d instantly profit $100 on&nbsp;
every Bitcoin you bought. And this is exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.320
Sam set out to do with his first company, Alameda&nbsp;
Research. After about a year of doing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.360
he realized that while arbitrage trading itself&nbsp;
was quite profitable, the real money was made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.400
by the exchanges who were earning $50 or a $100&nbsp;
per Bitcoin without taking on any of the risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:42.540
that Sam was. So, in early 2019, Sam set out to&nbsp;
create his own crypto exchange: FTX. But there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:48.300
was just one issue: he needed money. Fortunately&nbsp;
for Sam, there were plenty of VC firms who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.220
desperate to get into the crypto space including&nbsp;
some heavy hitters like SoftBank, Tiger Global,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:59.400
and even Blackstone. This has allowed them to&nbsp;
raise an impressive $1.8 billion over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.260
4 years. Sam would turn around and spend this&nbsp;
money on developing the firm, acquiring other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:10.080
crypto companies, and most important social media&nbsp;
marketing. They must’ve spent hundreds of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.660
on marketing because they were everywhere all&nbsp;
the time. And when you have so much exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.700
you’re bound to get customers whether&nbsp;
you’re good, bad, or otherwise. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:27.240
I don’t think Sam was bad per se, but it does seem&nbsp;
apparent that Sam was in it for the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:33.240
more than the crypto itself which is not exactly&nbsp;
what you wanna hear. We’ve seen time and time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.520
again massive engineering companies and tech&nbsp;
giants whether it be Intel, Boeing, and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.560
Apple fall flat on their face due to leaders who&nbsp;
didn’t quite have the technical understanding or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:48.720
belief in the product. And if that’s the case&nbsp;
with these juggernauts, it’s not surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:55.500
that it’s the case with FTX. But, not believing&nbsp;
in crypto was just the first of FTX’s issues.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.280
MISMANAGEMENT:&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.380
You would think that running a brokerage is&nbsp;
relatively straight forward if you have customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:11.220
You charge customers a fee for connecting buyers&nbsp;
and sellers, and it’s as simple as that. If FTX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:17.100
just stuck to this, they likely wouldn’t have&nbsp;
run into any issues at all. But, as you might’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:23.640
guessed, brokerages often get greedy and look for&nbsp;
um creative ways for generating extra revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:30.180
In the case of FTX, this can be traced back to 2&nbsp;
risky practices, the first of which being non cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:35.280
collateral. Whenever you have cash laying around&nbsp;
at a bank, brokerage, or really any financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.280
institution for that matter, it’s rarely kept as&nbsp;
cash. Usually, the institution will take the cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.440
and invest it to generate more revenue. They&nbsp;
figure that only a very small portion of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:52.020
customers will actually be withdrawing money at&nbsp;
any given time. So, they keep some cash collateral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:57.840
for this, but the rest gets invested. Usually,&nbsp;
most traditional institutions are forced by law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:05.100
to keep this collateral in cash, but when we’re&nbsp;
talking about crypto brokerages, the rules aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:11.340
as clear. Sam decided that it was a great idea to&nbsp;
keep this money not in US dollars, Euros, Pounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:18.900
or even Bitcoin. He decided to keep it in a token&nbsp;
that FTX themselves created called FTT. For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.240
longest time, this seemed like a brilliant play&nbsp;
given that the FTT token had rallied from just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:30.720
a few dollars to $70. But, the thing to note is&nbsp;
that such tokens not only outperform Bitcoin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:37.140
the upside but also to the downside, and let’s&nbsp;
just say Bitcoin hasn’t been performing so well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:43.260
This itself likely added significant pressure&nbsp;
onto Sam’s companies but the real crisis didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:48.720
come until people starting pointing this out on&nbsp;
the internet. For obvious reasons, FTX customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:55.020
started to feel a bit uneasy with FTX storing so&nbsp;
much money in the FTT token. And the nail in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:00.180
coffin came when Binance founder, CZ, announced&nbsp;
that they were going to liquidate all of their FTT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:06.720
within the next couple of months. This essentially&nbsp;
sparked a bank run FTT, causing FTT to fall back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:13.140
to where it started meaning that FTX’s collateral&nbsp;
was now worth a lot less. Ideally, FTX should just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:18.300
be able to sell their investments and make up for&nbsp;
any discrepancies in withdrawals but that brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.940
us into their second risky practice which includes&nbsp;
highly volatile leveraged investments. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.960
financial institutions invest their reserves&nbsp;
into highly liquid safe investments like money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:33.960
market funds, bonds, and mortgages. In the past,&nbsp;
we’ve seen that even that can lead to issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:39.480
so it’s no wonder why using these reserves&nbsp;
to engage in leveraged crypto trading led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:44.700
issues. And the worst part about all of this&nbsp;
is that financial stress often operates in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.200
negative feedback loops. When customers hear&nbsp;
that a given institution may be illiquid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:55.200
they get scared and withdraw their money. This&nbsp;
leads to the institution truly becoming illiquid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:01.380
even if they weren’t illiquid before. And given&nbsp;
that most of the influencers who pitched FTX are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.000
now telling investors to withdraw their money,&nbsp;
well if everything else didn’t already cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:15.180
a financial crisis at FTX, this definitely did.&nbsp;
And let’s just say it’s bad, it’s $8 billion bad.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:19.320
BAILOUT:&nbsp;

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:24.120
If the $8 billion deficit didn't convince&nbsp;
you that the FTX situation was bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.940
just listen to what Sam tried to do. Sam was so&nbsp;
desperate to get a bailout that he approached his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:36.000
biggest competitor Binance and asked them to&nbsp;
buy FTX out. At first, Binance agreed as this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.220
seemed like a slam dunk. Binance was already&nbsp;
the largest crypto brokerage by far in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.840
of trading volume and acquiring FTX would&nbsp;
just further solidify their position. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:51.960
it just took one look at FTX’s balance sheet&nbsp;
for Binance to realize that this company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.880
something that they’d like to stay far far away&nbsp;
from. Binance would make an official statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:02.640
that they would not pursue the FTX acquisition&nbsp;
due to mishandling of customer funds and alleged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.400
US agency investigations. And that brings us&nbsp;
to where we are today with Sam scrambling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:14.280
put together $8 billion which is by no means&nbsp;
an easy feat even for FTX. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:21.720
even at the peak, FTX was only worth $12.3&nbsp;
billion, so Sam is essentially asking investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:28.200
to pay 66% of their peak valuation just to bailout&nbsp;
the company. Who knows how much more money they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.720
need to survive this exodus of customers. From&nbsp;
an investor’s perspective, it makes no sense to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:39.540
pay so much money and voluntarily take on such a&nbsp;
massive gamble. In fact, existing investors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:45.300
already doing the exact opposite by writing off&nbsp;
FTX as a complete loss. Sequoia, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.200
sent out a letter to their partners stating&nbsp;
quote “Based on our current understanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.680
we are marking our investment down to $0.” And&nbsp;
if you can’t convince existing investors who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.120
already have skin in the game to invest more&nbsp;
money and save their investment, it’s gonna be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.820
virtually impossible to convince anyone else.&nbsp;
So, personally, I think that it’s just a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.800
of time until FTX does officially go bankrupt if&nbsp;
they haven’t already by the time you’re watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:16.320
this. And even if by some miracle they survive,&nbsp;
you probably don’t want to be keeping your crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.480
at an institution that doesn’t even believe in&nbsp;
crypto and now has a history of mismanagement.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:25.500
SEC CRACKDOWN:&nbsp;

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:30.660
Whether FTX does end up collapsing or not, you&nbsp;
can bet that regulators are going to use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.920
situation as justification to crackdown&nbsp;
on the crypto industry. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:40.560
that’s probably for the best. There’s a reason the&nbsp;
banks and traditional brokerages are regulated so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.080
heavily. It’s because if they aren’t, it usually&nbsp;
leads to end customers like you and me being hurt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:52.320
We’ve already seen two brokerages collapse this&nbsp;
year: Celsius and Voyager, and FTX may just be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:58.080
an addition to that list. And the truth is, while&nbsp;
I’m sure Sam is devastated that his company is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:03.360
the brink of collapse and that his net worth has&nbsp;
collapsed from $27 billion to not even a billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:08.940
it’s not like anything really changes for&nbsp;
him. He still has a 7.5% stake in Robinhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:15.540
and unlike FTX, Robinhood actually has cash.&nbsp;
In fact, Robinhood has $10 billion in cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:20.520
and their cash reserves are literally worth more&nbsp;
than their market cap. So, it’s more than likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:25.740
that Robinhood will survive this recession and see&nbsp;
the light of day at which point Sam will not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:31.440
become a billionaire again but a multi billionaire&nbsp;
and even a deca billionaire. The ones that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.360
truly left holding the bag are FTX’s 5 million&nbsp;
customers, Voyager’s 3.5 million customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:41.040
and Celsius’ nearly 2 million customers. I&nbsp;
mean, these three companies alone will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:46.920
affected over 10 million people and that’s just&nbsp;
the people affected by their direct fallout. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:52.020
we’re talking about indirect effects, it’s&nbsp;
likely tens of millions more. And if crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:58.380
really is the future, it appears to me that&nbsp;
regulation is a must, but that’s just what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:03.960
think. Do you agree? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you hope FTX customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:08.040
get their money back. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

